      "kin_ts": "transition state ‡",
      "phase_axis_p": "Pressure (Pa, log scale)",
      "phase_axis_t": "Temperature (K)",
      "phase_choose_condition": "Choose a real-world condition to mark on the diagram",
      "phase_choose_substance": "Choose a substance",
      "phase_co2_fus_note": "CO₂'s triple point sits at 5.1 atm. At ordinary room pressure there is no liquid band at all, which is why dry ice sublimes instead of melting.",
      "phase_critical": "critical point",
      "phase_diagram_title": "🗺 Where does it sit right now?",
      "phase_gas": "Gas",
      "phase_liquid": "Liquid",
      "phase_on_boundary": "right on the boundary",
      "phase_p_cart": "CO₂ cartridge, 20 °C",
      "phase_p_cart_n": "20 °C at about 57 atm. The cartridge sits ON the line, holding liquid and vapour together. That is why the pressure barely drops as it empties, until the last of the liquid is gone.",
      "phase_p_co2frz": "Home freezer",
      "phase_p_co2frz_n": "−18 °C, 1 atm. Still a gas. CO₂ needs −78.5 °C before it condenses at this pressure.",
      "phase_p_co2room": "Room, sea level",
      "phase_p_co2room_n": "25 °C, 1 atm. Gas, and nowhere near liquid.",
      "phase_p_cooker": "Pressure cooker",
      "phase_p_cooker_n": "121 °C at 2 atm. Same line, higher up. Sealing the pot lets pressure build, which drags the boiling point up with it, so the water reaches 121 °C instead of 100 °C and food cooks far faster.",
      "phase_p_decaf": "Decaffeinating coffee",
      "phase_p_decaf_n": "40 °C at 200 atm. Supercritical CO₂ seeps through beans like a gas but dissolves caffeine like a liquid, then simply evaporates away.",
      "phase_p_dryice": "Dry ice in the open",
      "phase_p_dryice_n": "−78.5 °C, 1 atm. Exactly on the sublimation line: solid turning straight into gas, no puddle.",
      "phase_p_everest": "Everest summit",
      "phase_p_everest_n": "71 °C at 0.33 atm. On the line again, but lower down it. Thin air means water boils at 71 °C, so a kettle up here physically cannot get hot enough to brew tea properly. The water is boiling and still too cool.",
      "phase_p_extinct": "Fire extinguisher",
      "phase_p_extinct_n": "20 °C at 84 atm. Compressed past the line into pure liquid. Release it and it flashes to gas, chilling so hard it makes CO₂ snow.",
      "phase_p_freeze": "Freeze-dryer",
      "phase_p_freeze_n": "−40 °C at 8 Pa. Below the triple-point pressure there is no liquid band, so ice goes straight to vapour. This is how freeze-dried food is made.",
      "phase_p_freezer": "Home freezer",
      "phase_p_freezer_n": "−18 °C, 1 atm. Solid, comfortably.",
      "phase_p_kettle": "Kettle boiling",
      "phase_p_kettle_n": "100 °C at 1 atm. Sitting exactly ON the vaporisation line, where liquid and vapour coexist. That is all \"boiling point\" means: the temperature where the line crosses your pressure. Now try Everest and the pressure cooker, which land on the same line at different heights.",
      "phase_p_room": "Room, sea level",
      "phase_p_room_n": "25 °C, 1 atm. A glass of water.",
      "phase_p_superc": "Supercritical steam",
      "phase_p_superc_n": "400 °C at 300 atm. Past the critical point liquid and gas stop being different things. Modern power-station boilers run here.",
      "phase_prompt": "Pick a condition above and watch where it lands. Try the same substance at the same temperature but two different pressures.",
      "phase_provenance": "Triple and critical points are measured values. The vaporisation curve is a Clausius–Clapeyron fit forced through three measured points (triple, a mid-range reference, critical), so it is exact at those three and within a few percent between them. The sublimation curve is a two-point fit. The melting line is drawn straight between two measured points, and the real one curves.",
      "phase_provenance_label": "Where these lines come from: ",
      "phase_solid": "Solid",
      "phase_sub_co2": "Carbon dioxide (CO₂)",
      "phase_sub_water": "Water (H₂O)",
      "phase_supercritical": "Supercritical fluid",
      "phase_supercritical_short": "Supercritical",
      "phase_triple": "triple point",
      "phase_water_fus_note": "Water's melting line leans LEFT: squeeze ice hard enough and it melts. Almost every other substance leans right. Push past a few thousand atmospheres and water stops making ordinary ice altogether and forms denser crystal types instead, which is where this straight line stops being a fair drawing.",
      "th_axis_g": "ΔG (kJ/mol)",
      "th_axis_t": "Temperature (K)",
      "th_badge_no": "not spontaneous",
      "th_badge_yes": "spontaneous",
      "th_case_haber": "Ammonia synthesis",
      "th_case_haber_why": "Four gas molecules collapse into two, so disorder falls. Below the crossover the energy release wins. This is the squeeze at the heart of the Haber process: cold favours yield, but cold is also slow, so industry compromises and adds pressure.",
      "th_case_lime": "Limestone to lime",
      "th_case_lime_why": "Uphill in energy, but it frees a gas out of a solid, so disorder climbs. Heat it past the crossover and the entropy term takes over. Real lime kilns run a little above this line.",
      "th_case_ozone": "Making ozone from oxygen",
      "th_case_ozone_why": "Uphill in energy AND down in disorder. Heat cannot rescue it. The ozone layer exists because ultraviolet photons pay the bill, not because the reaction wants to happen.",
      "th_case_perox": "Peroxide decomposing",
      "th_case_perox_why": "Downhill in energy and up in disorder at the same time. Nothing can stop it; only the missing catalyst keeps the bottle stable on the shelf.",
      "th_flips_at": "Flips at T = ΔH/ΔS = ",
      "th_map_intro": "ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is a straight line in T whose slope is −ΔS. Below the axis means it happens on its own. Two sign combinations never cross the axis; the other two always do, and the crossing point is just T = ΔH / ΔS.",
      "th_map_title": "🌡 Temperature decides. Drag it and watch two of the four lines change their mind.",
      "th_never_flips": "Never flips: both terms point the same way.",
      "th_not_spont": "ΔG > 0 · will not happen on its own",
      "th_preset_furnace": "Furnace",
      "th_preset_haber": "Ammonia crossover",
      "th_preset_ice": "Ice point",
      "th_preset_kiln": "Lime kiln crossover",
      "th_preset_room": "Room",
      "th_provenance": "ΔH and ΔS here are standard-state values measured at 298 K, and the straight lines assume they hold steady as the temperature climbs. They drift a little in reality, so treat a crossover temperature as a good estimate rather than an exact threshold. Spontaneous also does not mean fast: peroxide is spontaneous at every temperature on this chart and still sits in the bottle for months.",
      "th_provenance_label": "Reading the fine print: ",
      "th_q_always": "ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0 · spontaneous at every temperature",
      "th_q_high": "ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0 · spontaneous only when hot",
      "th_q_low": "ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0 · spontaneous only when cold",
      "th_q_never": "ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0 · never spontaneous",
      "th_spont": "ΔG < 0 · spontaneous",
      "th_temp_label": "Temperature",
